/**
\defgroup distrib_generic Generic routes
\brief Generic numerical routes and compile-time dispatchers for probability distributions.

This module provides generic numerical routes and compile-time dispatchers for probability distributions (`distrib`s), covering cumulative distribution functions, quantiles, expectations, moments, expected Fisher information, and random variate generation. Its declarations live in `<laplace/distrib/generic_cdf.hpp>`, `<laplace/distrib/generic_quantile.hpp>`, `<laplace/distrib/generic_expectation.hpp>`, `<laplace/distrib/generic_info.hpp>`, and `<laplace/distrib/generic_rng.hpp>` inside namespace `laplace::distrib`, so users call them as `laplace::distrib::NAME`. All functions are marked `LAPLACE_HD` and execute on both host and device inside Kokkos kernels. On the tested pilot distributions, generic routes achieve 31.9 conditioning units on continuous quantiles, exact ranks on discrete search, 5.91 ulp of \f$\max(|\log|, 1)\f$ on discrete log-CDFs, 6.7 eps on moments, and 4.0 eps on smooth continuous expected information.

## Interface and dispatch

Generic routes operate on any distribution type `D` satisfying the core interface documented in \ref distrib_core. Every `D` must provide `D::kind` (`continuous` or `discrete`), `D::n_params` and `D::params`, `logpdf(y)` or `logpmf(y)`, `support_lower()`, `support_upper()`, `center()`, and `scale()`. Continuous distributions with non-smooth points in the observation variable optionally provide `y_kinks()`. Computing expected Fisher information and its parameter derivatives of order \f$K \in \{0, \dots, 4\}\f$ also requires `logpdf_d1(y)` through `logpdf_dN(y)` (or `logpmf_d1(y)` through `logpmf_dN(y)`), with \f$N = K + 2\f$ in the Hessian form and \f$N = K + 1\f$ in the score form.

Eight dispatchers use C++20 `requires` clauses to invoke `D`'s specialized member function when present and fall back to the generic route otherwise, with zero run-time overhead. These dispatchers are \ref laplace::distrib::distrib_cdf "distrib_cdf()", \ref laplace::distrib::distrib_ccdf "distrib_ccdf()", \ref laplace::distrib::distrib_log_cdf "distrib_log_cdf()", \ref laplace::distrib::distrib_log_ccdf "distrib_log_ccdf()", \ref laplace::distrib::distrib_quantile "distrib_quantile()", \ref laplace::distrib::distrib_quantile_from_log "distrib_quantile_from_log()", \ref laplace::distrib::distrib_quantile_from_log_upper "distrib_quantile_from_log_upper()", and \ref laplace::distrib::distrib_expected_info "distrib_expected_info()". Each generic route can also be invoked directly by name. For tensor packing, parameter descriptors, and specialized distribution implementations, see \ref distrib_core, \ref distrib_gaussian1, \ref distrib_laplace1, \ref distrib_poisson, and \ref distrib_gamma1.

## Definitions

Let \f$F(y)\f$ denote the cumulative distribution function at observation \f$y\f$, \f$f(y)\f$ the continuous density, \f$p(i)\f$ the discrete probability mass at integer \f$i\f$, and \f$l(y; \theta) = \log f(y; \theta)\f$ (or \f$\log p(y; \theta)\f$) the log-density or log-mass with natural parameters \f$\theta = (\theta_0, \dots, \theta_{P-1})\f$.

For discrete distributions with \f$k = \lfloor y \rfloor\f$, \ref laplace::distrib::log_cdf_by_sum "log_cdf_by_sum()" and \ref laplace::distrib::cdf_by_sum "cdf_by_sum()" compute \f$\log F(y) = \log \sum_{i \le k} p(i)\f$ and \f$F(y)\f$. Similarly, \ref laplace::distrib::log_ccdf_by_sum "log_ccdf_by_sum()" and \ref laplace::distrib::ccdf_by_sum "ccdf_by_sum()" compute \f$\log(1 - F(y)) = \log \sum_{i > k} p(i)\f$ and \f$1 - F(y)\f$.

For target probability \f$p \in (0, 1)\f$, continuous quantile routes (\ref laplace::distrib::quantile_by_root "quantile_by_root()", \ref laplace::distrib::quantile_by_root_from_log "quantile_by_root_from_log()", and \ref laplace::distrib::quantile_by_root_from_log_upper "quantile_by_root_from_log_upper()") solve \f$F(y) = p\f$ from \f$p\f$, \f$\log p\f$, or \f$\log q = \log(1 - p)\f$. Discrete quantile routes (\ref laplace::distrib::quantile_by_search "quantile_by_search()", \ref laplace::distrib::quantile_by_search_from_log "quantile_by_search_from_log()", and \ref laplace::distrib::quantile_by_search_from_log_upper "quantile_by_search_from_log_upper()") return the smallest integer \f$k\f$ in the support satisfying \f$F(k) \ge p\f$.

Given an integrand functor \f$h\f$, \ref laplace::distrib::expectation_by_integral "expectation_by_integral()" computes \f$\mathbb{E}[h(Y)] = \int h(y) f(y) \, \mathrm{d}y\f$ for continuous distributions or \f$\sum_y h(y) p(y)\f$ for discrete distributions. For integer order \f$k \ge 0\f$ and shift `about`, \ref laplace::distrib::moment_by_integral "moment_by_integral()" evaluates \f$\mathbb{E}[(Y - \mathrm{about})^k]\f$.

For derivative order \f$K \in \{0, \dots, 4\}\f$ and sorted parameter index multiset \f$C = (c_1, \dots, c_K)\f$, \ref laplace::distrib::expected_info_by_integral "expected_info_by_integral()" computes the expected Fisher information matrix \f$I_{ab}(\theta)\f$ for \f$K = 0\f$ or its parameter derivative \f$\partial^K I_{ab}(\theta) / \partial\theta_C\f$ for \f$K \ge 1\f$, reaching total derivative order 6. The enumeration \ref laplace::distrib::info_form "info_form" selects the formulation: the Hessian form sets \f$I_{ab} = -\mathbb{E}[l_{ab}]\f$, whereas the score form sets \f$I_{ab} = \mathbb{E}[l_a l_b]\f$ (Lehmann and Casella, 1998). Differentiating the moving probability measure \f$f(y; \theta)\,\mathrm{d}y\f$ gives
\f[
\frac{\partial^K}{\partial \theta_C} I_{ab} = -\mathbb{E}\left[ \sum_{T \subseteq C} l_{ab + (C \setminus T)} B_T \right]
\f]
in the Hessian form (`info_form::hessian`), and
\f[
\frac{\partial^K}{\partial \theta_C} I_{ab} = \mathbb{E}\left[ \sum_{T \subseteq C} S_{C \setminus T} B_T \right], \qquad S_U = \sum_{V \subseteq U} l_{a + V} l_{b + (U \setminus V)}
\f]
in the score form (`info_form::score`). Here \f$T\f$ ranges over sub-multisets of \f$C\f$, \f$l_U\f$ is the parameter derivative of \f$l\f$ over multiset \f$U\f$, \f$ab + U\f$ denotes \f$\{a, b\} \cup U\f$, and \f$B_T = e^{-l} \partial_T e^l\f$ (with \f$B_\emptyset = 1\f$) is the complete Bell polynomial (Bell, 1934) of the derivatives of \f$l\f$ over \f$T\f$.

Random variates are drawn by \ref laplace::distrib::rng_by_inversion "rng_by_inversion()" via quantile inversion \f$Y = F^{-1}(u)\f$, where \f$u\f$ is generated by \ref laplace::distrib::open_unit_uniform "open_unit_uniform()" from the top 53 bits \f$n\f$ of a 64-bit unsigned integer `gen.urand64()` as \f$u = (n + 1/2) 2^{-53}\f$.

## Domain and special values

For discrete cumulative functions, if \f$\lfloor y \rfloor\f$ is below `support_lower()`, then \f$F(y) = 0\f$, \f$\log F(y) = -\infty\f$, and \f$\log(1 - F(y)) = 0\f$. If \f$\lfloor y \rfloor\f$ is at least `support_upper()`, then \f$F(y) = 1\f$, \f$\log F(y) = 0\f$, and \f$\log(1 - F(y)) = -\infty\f$.

Quantile routes return `support_lower()` at \f$p = 0\f$, \f$\log p \le -\mathrm{distrib\_infinity}\f$, or \f$\log q = 0\f$, and return `support_upper()` at \f$p = 1\f$, \f$\log p = 0\f$, or \f$\log q \le -\mathrm{distrib\_infinity}\f$. Out-of-domain probabilities (\f$p < 0\f$, \f$p > 1\f$, \f$\log p > 0\f$, \f$\log q > 0\f$) and NaN inputs return quiet NaN.

In \ref laplace::distrib::expectation_by_integral "expectation_by_integral()", non-finite integrand terms or non-convergence set `converged = false` in the returned `quad_result`; \ref laplace::distrib::moment_by_integral "moment_by_integral()" returns quiet NaN when integration fails to converge. In \ref laplace::distrib::expected_info_by_integral "expected_info_by_integral()", any component that fails to converge or violates the finite-regularity condition below evaluates to quiet NaN.

## Numerical methods

Each generic route operates in logarithmic coordinates or splits integrals at non-smooth points to preserve tail precision.

### Quantile search and root finding

Both continuous and discrete quantile routes solve in the tail holding the target probability: \f$\log F(y) = \log p\f$ for \f$p \le 1/2\f$ (or \f$\log p \le -\log 2\f$), and \f$\log(1 - F(y)) = \log(1 - p)\f$ otherwise. For continuous distributions, root finding solves for the root of the strictly increasing function
\f[
g(y) = \begin{cases} \log F(y) - t, & \text{lower tail, } t = \log p \le -\log 2, \\ t - \log(1 - F(y)), & \text{upper tail, } t = \log(1 - p). \end{cases}
\f]
Starting from \f$x = \mathrm{center}()\f$, bracketing doubles steps of size \f$\mathrm{scale}()\f$ towards an infinite boundary or takes geometric steps \f$(e - x) 2^{-m}\f$ with \f$m = 1, 2, 4, 8, \dots\f$ towards a finite boundary \f$e\f$. Guarded Newton iteration (Dennis and Schnabel, 1996) using \f$g'(y) = f(y)/F(y)\f$ (or \f$f(y)/(1 - F(y))\f$) falls back to bisection when a Newton step leaves the bracket, using the geometric mean of boundary distances when they differ by more than a factor of 4. A final linear-scale Newton step on \f$F(y) = p\f$ (or \f$1 - F(y) = 1 - p\f$) removes logarithmic tail rounding error. For discrete distributions, integer search brackets from \f$\lfloor \mathrm{center}() \rfloor\f$ using doubling steps of \f$\lceil \mathrm{scale}() \rceil\f$ and bisects integers until the bracket width is 1.

### Discrete summation and continuous quadrature

For \f$k < \mathrm{center}()\f$, discrete cumulative routines sum \f$p(i)\f$ downwards from \f$i_{\mathrm{start}} = k\f$ and obtain \f$\log(1 - F(k))\f$ from \f$\log(1 - e^{\log F(k)})\f$. For \f$k \ge \mathrm{center}()\f$, they sum \f$p(i)\f$ upwards from \f$i_{\mathrm{start}} = k + 1\f$ and compute \f$\log F(k)\f$ from the complement. Each sum factors out its leading term as \f$l(i_{\mathrm{start}}) + \log\left(\sum e^{l(i) - l(i_{\mathrm{start}})}\right)\f$ to prevent underflow, stopping at a support boundary or by a geometric tail bound valid for log-concave masses (Bagnoli and Bergstrom, 2005).

For continuous expectations, \ref laplace::distrib::expectation_by_integral "expectation_by_integral()" integrates \f$h(y) e^{\log f(y)}\f$ with \ref laplace::math::quad::panels_quad "panels_quad()" over the support, split at `center()` and `y_kinks()` with initial panel width `scale()`. For discrete expectations, it sweeps upwards and downwards from \f$\lfloor \mathrm{center}() \rfloor\f$, stopping each direction at the support boundary or by a two-term geometric tail bound once away from the centre with decreasing mass.

### Expected information and finite regularity

The default format returned by \ref laplace::distrib::generic_info_form "generic_info_form()" is `info_form::score` for every distribution. It is the only form valid under finite parameter regularity, matches the Hessian form in accuracy on smooth test distributions, and avoids cancellation in \f$-\mathbb{E}[l_{aa}]\f$ that \f$\mathbb{E}[l_a^2]\f$ does not suffer. All components of \f$I\f$ and its derivatives are evaluated in a single pass on shared quadrature nodes or discrete summation terms, refining until every component converges.

When a parameter \f$\theta_j\f$ has finite regularity \f$r_j\f$ (\ref laplace::distrib::distrib_param_info::regularity), \f$l(y; \theta)\f$ has a moving kink in \f$y\f$. Pointwise differentiation omits Dirac or boundary terms unless \f$n_a + n_b + n_C \le r_j + 1\f$ in the Hessian form, or \f$n_C = 0\f$ or \f$\max(n_a, n_b) + n_C \le r_j + 1\f$ in the score form, where \f$n_a, n_b, n_C\f$ count index \f$j\f$ in \f$a, b, C\f$. For `laplace` (\f$r_\mu = 0\f$), the score form gives \f$I\f$ and derivatives with at most one \f$\mu\f$ in \f$C\f$ and none in \f$(a, b)\f$, whereas the Hessian form returns quiet NaN for \f$I_{\mu\mu}\f$.

### Uniform sources for random variates

Because \ref laplace::distrib::open_unit_uniform "open_unit_uniform()" produces \f$u \in [2^{-54}, 1 - 2^{-54}]\f$ symmetric about \f$1/2\f$, quantile inversion is never evaluated at \f$p = 0\f$ or \f$p = 1\f$ and operates on \f$1 - u\f$ directly when \f$u > 1/2\f$. The default \ref laplace::distrib::counter_uniform_source "counter_uniform_source" implements SplitMix64 (Steele, Lea, and Flood, 2014) at counter state \f$z = \mathrm{seed} + ((\mathrm{index} \cdot 2^{32}) + i)\gamma\f$ with \f$\gamma = \mathtt{0x9e3779b97f4a7c15}\f$, making draws depend only on `seed`, `index`, and \f$i\f$ across threads and backends. Any generator exposing `urand64()`, such as `Kokkos::Random_XorShift64_Pool` or `Kokkos::Random_XorShift1024_Pool`, can be supplied instead.

## Accuracy

See \ref accuracy "How accuracy is measured" for error metrics and \ref accuracy_inverse for the conditioning scale of continuous quantiles. All tests compare generic routes against hidden closed-form implementations on gcc, and CUDA runs match host accuracy within the same bounds.

| Route | Tested distributions | Measured maximum (gcc) | Test bound |
|---|---|---|---|
| Continuous quantile | `gaussian1`, `laplace`, `gamma1` (\f$p \in [10^{-300}, 1 - 10^{-15}]\f$) | 31.9 (`gamma1(2, 0.3)`, \f$p = 10^{-300}\f$) | 8.0 (`gaussian1`, `laplace`), 64.0 (`gamma1`) |
| Discrete quantile | `poisson` (\f$\mu \in [0.05, 10^4]\f$) | Exact smallest integer \f$k\f$ | Exact match (ties within \f$10^{-13}\f$ skipped) |
| Discrete log-CDF | `poisson` (\f$\mu \in [0.05, 1000]\f$) | 0.60–5.91 ulp of \f$\max(|\log|, 1)\f$ | 16.0 ulp |
| Moments | `gaussian1`, `laplace`, `gamma1`, `poisson` | 1.8 (`gaussian1`, `laplace`), 3.0 (`gamma1`), 0.6–6.7 (`poisson`) eps | 64.0 (`gaussian1`, `laplace`, `gamma1`), 16.0 (`poisson`) eps |
| Expected information | `gaussian1`, `gamma1` (\f$K = 0, \dots, 4\f$) | 4.0 eps (Hessian), 3.2 eps (score) | 16.0 eps |
| Expected information | `poisson` (\f$\mu \in \{0.05, 3.5\}\f$, \f$K = 0, \dots, 4\f$) | 4.7 eps (Hessian), 10.9 eps (score) | 16.0 eps (Hessian), 24.0 eps (score) |
| Expected information | `poisson(40)` (\f$K = 0, \dots, 4\f$) | 247 eps (Hessian), 116 eps (score) | 400.0 eps (Hessian), 200.0 eps (score) |
| Expected information | `laplace(0.4, 1.3)` (score, non-NaN) | Within test bounds | 64 eps (\f$K = 0, 1\f$), 256 eps (\f$K = 2\f$), 1024 eps (\f$K = 4\f$) |

At \f$\log q \in \{-50, -300\}\f$, upper-tail quantiles are additionally checked by comparing \f$\log(1 - F)\f$ at the result against \f$\log q\f$ with relative tolerance \f$10^{-12}\f$. Full per-order accuracy tables are recorded in `docs/numerics.md`.

## Speed

Generic quantiles, discrete cumulative functions, and random draws run in tens to thousands of nanoseconds per call, whereas one-pass generic expected information evaluates log-density derivatives at every quadrature node or summation term; full timings on CPU (one gcc thread and OpenMP) and CUDA (RTX 4070 SUPER) are recorded in `docs/numerics.md`.

## Example

The following example generates pseudorandom draws and evaluates the expected Fisher information matrix inside a Kokkos parallel kernel:

\code{.cpp}
#include <laplace/distrib/gamma1.hpp>
#include <laplace/distrib/generic_info.hpp>
#include <laplace/distrib/generic_rng.hpp>
#include <Kokkos_Core.hpp>
#include <cstdint>

void sample_and_info(Kokkos::View<double*>& draws,
                     Kokkos::View<double[3]>& info,
                     int n,
                     std::uint64_t seed) {
  const laplace::distrib::gamma1_distrib d{2.0, 0.3};

  Kokkos::parallel_for("draw_samples", n, KOKKOS_LAMBDA(const int i) {
    draws(i) = laplace::distrib::rng_by_inversion(d, seed, static_cast<std::uint64_t>(i));
  });

  Kokkos::parallel_for("compute_info", 1, KOKKOS_LAMBDA(const int) {
    const auto I = laplace::distrib::distrib_expected_info<0>(d);
    info(0) = I[0];
    info(1) = I[1];
    info(2) = I[2];
  });
}
\endcode

## Module functions

Functions and types in this module are defined in `laplace::distrib` and called as `laplace::distrib::NAME`.

### Expectations and moments
- \ref laplace::distrib::expectation_by_integral "expectation_by_integral()" — expectation \f$\mathbb{E}[h(Y)]\f$ by numerical quadrature or summation
- \ref laplace::distrib::moment_by_integral "moment_by_integral()" — raw or central moment \f$\mathbb{E}[(Y - \mathrm{about})^k]\f$

### Expected information
- \ref laplace::distrib::info_form "info_form" — representation format (Hessian or score)
- \ref laplace::distrib::expected_info_result "expected_info_result" — return type for information matrix and derivative tensors
- \ref laplace::distrib::expected_info_by_integral "expected_info_by_integral()" — expected information and derivatives by numerical quadrature or summation
- \ref laplace::distrib::generic_info_form "generic_info_form()" — default representation (`info_form::score`)
- \ref laplace::distrib::distrib_expected_info "distrib_expected_info()" — expected information dispatching to member or generic route

### Discrete cumulative distribution functions
- \ref laplace::distrib::log_cdf_by_sum "log_cdf_by_sum()" — natural logarithm of cumulative distribution function by summation
- \ref laplace::distrib::log_ccdf_by_sum "log_ccdf_by_sum()" — natural logarithm of complementary cumulative distribution function by summation
- \ref laplace::distrib::cdf_by_sum "cdf_by_sum()" — cumulative distribution function by summation
- \ref laplace::distrib::ccdf_by_sum "ccdf_by_sum()" — complementary cumulative distribution function by summation
- \ref laplace::distrib::distrib_cdf "distrib_cdf()" — cumulative distribution function dispatching to member or generic summation
- \ref laplace::distrib::distrib_ccdf "distrib_ccdf()" — complementary cumulative distribution function dispatching to member or generic summation
- \ref laplace::distrib::distrib_log_cdf "distrib_log_cdf()" — natural logarithm of cumulative distribution function dispatching to member or generic summation
- \ref laplace::distrib::distrib_log_ccdf "distrib_log_ccdf()" — natural logarithm of complementary cumulative distribution function dispatching to member or generic summation

### Quantiles
- \ref laplace::distrib::quantile_by_root "quantile_by_root()" — continuous quantile \f$F^{-1}(p)\f$ by root finding
- \ref laplace::distrib::quantile_by_root_from_log "quantile_by_root_from_log()" — continuous quantile from log-probability \f$\log p\f$
- \ref laplace::distrib::quantile_by_root_from_log_upper "quantile_by_root_from_log_upper()" — continuous upper quantile from complementary log-probability \f$\log(1 - p)\f$
- \ref laplace::distrib::quantile_by_search "quantile_by_search()" — discrete quantile by integer search
- \ref laplace::distrib::quantile_by_search_from_log "quantile_by_search_from_log()" — discrete quantile from log-probability \f$\log p\f$
- \ref laplace::distrib::quantile_by_search_from_log_upper "quantile_by_search_from_log_upper()" — discrete upper quantile from complementary log-probability \f$\log(1 - p)\f$
- \ref laplace::distrib::distrib_quantile "distrib_quantile()" — quantile dispatching to member or generic route
- \ref laplace::distrib::distrib_quantile_from_log "distrib_quantile_from_log()" — quantile from \f$\log p\f$ dispatching to member or generic route
- \ref laplace::distrib::distrib_quantile_from_log_upper "distrib_quantile_from_log_upper()" — upper quantile from \f$\log(1 - p)\f$ dispatching to member or generic route

### Random number generation
- \ref laplace::distrib::counter_uniform_source "counter_uniform_source" — SplitMix64 counter-based uniform generator keyed by seed and index
- \ref laplace::distrib::open_unit_uniform "open_unit_uniform()" — open unit interval uniform draw in \f$[2^{-54}, 1 - 2^{-54}]\f$
- \ref laplace::distrib::rng_by_inversion(const D&, Gen&) "rng_by_inversion(d, gen)" — random variate by quantile inversion using an explicit generator
- \ref laplace::distrib::rng_by_inversion(const D&, std::uint64_t, std::uint64_t) "rng_by_inversion(d, seed, index)" — random variate by quantile inversion using the default counter source

